_irNotes: {
	"decision-card-description-locale": "A picture is drawn from the reader's words, for the deck's audience The text levels once wrote each card an English picture description and a reader-facing one; the first was the image prompt and the second prefilled the edit sheet, so model prose the reader never wrote decided what was drawn. They now write the cards' words alone, and the sheet starts empty. illustrate-card asks for Subject: a single <word>. Context: <title>; <details>. from the card's word and the deck's title, details and custom audience, with a redraw's description, then the audience's style and a white background. Level 3 draws on Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite Image from the words as written: Phoenix drew a paca as an alpaca and a giant anteater as a mongoose whatever the prompt said. Level 2's model reads English alone, so the cheap text tier translates the words there, and the reader's word stays beside its translation. The trials and the rejected options are in the archived record docs/archive/2026-10-31-image-prompt.md. No prompt is seeded with examples drawn from a deck a test generates"
	"decision-card-redraw-sheet": "A card is edited in a bottom sheet that redraws its level 3 A ✏️ on the card in view (explore and the card route, owned decks only, where the delete control shows) opens a sheet the app styles, a popover like #draft-loc-menu: the card's current image, one field \"Detalhes desta imagem\", a \"Gerar de novo\" button and a \"Pronto\" button that closes it. \"Gerar de novo\" is a form update of the card's Flashcard row: its description and redraw: drawing. CardRedrawMachine takes settled → drawing only while the card's level-3 CardArt row has settled, and that transition restarts the row as a new run of the effect: redraws grows by one, which is part of illustrate-card's key and of its seed, so the same text twice is still a new drawing. The row carries the reader's description; drawn, the image replaces the card's art, and either outcome settles the card back. The sheet's bar reads the row's state with colour alone, as the generation bars do, and a refused or unanswered drawing shows the no-art frame in the sheet while the card keeps the art it had. The reader may redraw again as soon as it settles. Rejected: a new request entity beside the card (a second row to keep in step with the card it describes), and a new deck run for one card (it would move DeckLevel's run and make the tab read the deck's text as unsettled)"
	"decision-deck-owner": "A deck is its owner's; a favourite each reader's; a translation everyone's Which decks were a reader's lived in a tab-only list, so a reload, a new tab or a pasted link made every deck someone else's and hid its ✏️ from its own author, while the server let anyone write any deck: card_set and flashcard were public writes, and one card_set.favorite field was every visitor's favourite. A deck and its cards now carry owner, the signed-in reader (a guest is one, with an id the server minted), stamped by auth_uid(); anyone reads them and only the owner writes them (020_owner_writes.sql, since #Access has no such mode). The browser asks the same question of Me, the reader's one visible row. A favourite is each reader's own Favorite, counted in DeckStats, as realworld counts an article's. Translations, which every reader wrote into the cards' hints, are DeckTranslation and CardTranslation, a cache any reader writes, so reading someone else's deck in another language writes nothing of theirs. Promoting a guest keeps the person's id, so their decks stay theirs. Rejected: ownership by device key (the deck already names it, but a person on a second browser would own nothing), and keeping the tab's list in device storage (still one browser's, and the server would go on accepting anyone's writes)"
	"decision-deck-title-details": "A deck is created from a title and optional details The form has two fields. \"Título da coleção\" is short and becomes the deck's name (topic on the draft, name on the CardSet). \"Detalhes (opcional)\" refines the text effects' prompt for every card and is never shown as the title; it rides the draft and the deck (details), so a level started from the deck's rows asks the same thing"
	"decision-delete-two-taps": "A card is deleted by two taps on the same button The 🗑 on a card of a deck this tab generated is one button. The first tap arms it: it turns into a red \"Apagar?\" button, localized, and a second tap within about four seconds deletes the card; otherwise it reverts to 🗑. There is no confirmation box and no extra text. The arming is a terminal chart over a tab row, card_delete, one per card: idle → armed on a click, armed → idle after 4000 ms on the terminal's clock, armed → confirmed on the second click. The revert is the chart's after, not CSS: a CSS clock is rejected by omnishell (reduced motion and background tabs run it at the wrong speed, and ?clock=manual cannot hold it), and a hand-written timer has no place in a handler. The button keeps its focus across both taps, so a keyboard reader presses it twice, and its name moves from \"Apagar carta\" to \"Apagar?\" inside a polite live region, which a screen reader announces. A confirmed row is an intent row. create_set, seated on card_delete with data-on-mutation, applies it as before: owned decks only, never the deck's last card, the deck's count and the session's position follow. A refused intent drops the row, which returns the button to 🗑"
	"decision-draft-per-device": "The form and the text slots are the device's The form was one public row every visitor shared with the slots beside it: a fresh browser showed another reader's title and details, and a second writer reverted a reader's card count. The unsent form is now the browser's GenerationForm, durability device, never synced, and its attached chart observes the deck's DeckProgress row. It was the tab's at first, and a reload mid-generation then forgot the deck it was generating while the server went on drawing it; the tabs of one browser now share the form, which is the reader's one draft whichever tab shows it. The server's rows stay server-side so a deck finishes with the tab closed, partitioned by a device key: create_set mints it once from two of the terminal's draws into Device, with the device's two slots <key>:draft-cheap and <key>:draft-heavy, and a deck names its device in card_set.device. The deck's id ends in the key too: a title alone made two devices titling a deck alike write one deck. Rejected: a server form row per reader (it would sync typed text only this browser needs), keying the form by tab (a reload loses it), and keying the slots by tab (a deck's slots must outlive the tab that asked)"
	"decision-escape-hatches": "Escape hatches window-print calls the browser's print dialog; card-art-fan-out writes one art row per card, and generation-slot-bootstrap restores a device slot, and deck-advance dispatches the source facts. ai-browser runs a model in a worker. Each is where replay stops"
	"decision-generate-preview-binds-draft": "The /generate preview shows the deck being generated The preview bound play_session, which create_set would not move while a generation was unsettled, so it went on showing a deck explored before. It now binds the form's row draft (generation_form): its set_id and that deck's cards, showing the session's card only when the session is on that deck and the deck's first card otherwise. The session-healing code that moved the session to the generated deck is gone"
	"decision-generation-in-machines": "Generation continues with the browser closed Query actors observe card, draft and artwork facts and deliver events to durable Cortex charts in the same transaction. The form attaches to its deck's progress while the screen is open; it does not own the server work"
}

